# Direct dependencies of the API gateway image.
#
# This file carries the constraints; `requirements-api.txt`, generated from it, carries the full
# pinned closure -- that is the one the Dockerfile installs. Never edit the second by hand.
#
# The image contains neither torch nor transformers: generation is delegated to the vLLM server.
# It does carry Presidio and the spaCy models, because the audit log must be anonymised on the
# service side -- it is the only place in the system where real patient data would settle
# durably. That choice costs about 400 MB of image; the alternative, a degraded anonymisation in
# the container and a complete one elsewhere, would make production and development diverge.
#
# Regenerate after every `uv lock`:
#
#   uv export --frozen --no-emit-project --all-groups --no-hashes #     --format requirements-txt | grep -E '^[a-zA-Z]' | sed -E 's/\+cu[0-9]+$//' #     > /tmp/constraints.txt
#   uv pip compile infra/requirements-api.in --python-version 3.12 #     --python-platform x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --constraint /tmp/constraints.txt #     --no-header -o infra/requirements-api.txt
#
# Constraining by the project lock file is not a detail: without it, the image resolution drifts
# from the repository's, and the deployed image no longer holds the versions continuous
# integration audits.

fastapi==0.138.2
uvicorn[standard]==0.49.0
httpx==0.28.1
pydantic==2.13.4
presidio-analyzer==2.2.363
# 2.2.363 locks `cryptography` under the version that fixes the 2026 CVE on certificate
# chain verification; 2.2.362 lets the update through. Presidio's encryption operator is not
# used here, but a vulnerable dependency in the image is one all the same.
presidio-anonymizer==2.2.362
cryptography==50.0.1
spacy==3.8.13

